00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:23.958
Friday at 9.45 in the morning in Ottawa, the Supreme Court of Canada is due to rule on six

00:06:23.958 --> 00:06:27.678
appeals of Bill 21, Quebec's 2019 secularism law.

00:06:28.018 --> 00:06:33.038
The law bars many public sector workers, teachers and police among them from wearing religious

00:06:33.038 --> 00:06:35.338
symbols such as a hijab or a cross at work.

00:06:35.958 --> 00:06:39.818
Quebec protected it with section 33 of the charter, the notwithstanding clause.

00:06:40.278 --> 00:06:44.598
The question before the judges is whether that shield holds and whether the law can be struck

00:06:44.598 --> 00:06:44.978
down.

00:06:45.498 --> 00:06:50.218
Appellants, including Muslim, Sikh and Catholic women who teach, want the law struck down.

00:06:50.218 --> 00:06:55.838
Their strongest point is that sections 23 and 28 on minority language education in gender

00:06:55.838 --> 00:07:01.558
equality cannot be overridden by the notwithstanding clause and cover people-to-law effects. Critics

00:07:01.558 --> 00:07:06.078
say the statute is aimed at minority women in a province with deep Catholic roots. In

00:07:06.078 --> 00:07:12.558
April 2021, after a 33-day trial, Quebec Superior Court Justice Mark Andre Blanchard upheld

00:07:12.558 --> 00:07:17.098
the law, and he wrote that it forced a cruel consequence that dehumanizes the people targeted.

00:07:17.098 --> 00:07:21.558
He said the notwithstanding clause stopped him from striking it down for charter violations.

00:07:22.058 --> 00:07:26.178
The Quebec Court of Appeal upheld it in February 2024 for the same reason.

00:07:26.598 --> 00:07:32.238
At the Supreme Court hearing in March, one lawyer for an appellant warned of a mini-Trump in Canada using the clause.

00:07:32.918 --> 00:07:38.758
Quebec's strongest answer is that the clause was the bargain that made the charter possible and that the province used it as written.

00:07:38.758 --> 00:07:43.938
John Christophe Jesman, Quebec director of the Christian think tank cartus, told the

00:07:43.938 --> 00:07:48.458
Globe and Mail the law has broad support and that state secularism is a cherished value

00:07:48.458 --> 00:07:48.718
there.

00:07:49.158 --> 00:07:53.538
He said many nationalists think the Quebec nation can flourish inside the Canadian constitutional

00:07:53.538 --> 00:07:58.498
order and that a very restrictive decision would hand ammunition to separatist nationalists.

00:07:58.958 --> 00:08:03.518
Former liberal premier Jean Charest said the party Quebec was hoping the ruling will

00:08:03.518 --> 00:08:04.258
be controversial.

00:08:04.258 --> 00:08:09.298
The Globe reports that premiere Paul St. Pierre Plamondone's minority government, elected

00:08:09.298 --> 00:08:14.378
with 28% of the vote, a record low, would likely welcome a decision that limits the clause

00:08:14.378 --> 00:08:16.418
or invalidates parts of Bill 21.

00:08:17.058 --> 00:08:21.358
On the last day of the hearing, Chief Justice Richard Wagner said he did not think the case

00:08:21.358 --> 00:08:24.378
would be disposed of by extremist or catastrophic scenarios.

00:08:24.978 --> 00:08:26.618
The judgment is set for Friday morning.

